      "violationName": "Missing Table Caption",
      "category": "semantic",
      "html": "<table>",
      "target": null,
      "description": "The table lacks a caption, which is required to provide context for the data presented. This can be particularly problematic for users who rely on screen readers or other assistive technologies.",
      "impact": "serious",
      "confidence": 1.0
    }
  ]
}

{
  "violations": [
    {
      "wcagCode": "G146",
      "violationName": "Image with No Alternative Text",
      "category": "semantic",
      "html": "<img src=\"/WAI/content-assets/wcag-act-rules/test-assets/shared/w3c-logo.png\" role=\"presentation\">",
      "target": null,
      "description": "The image has a 'role' attribute set to 'presentation', which suggests it is not intended for visual rendering. However, there is no alternative text provided, which violates WCAG 2.0 Success Criterion G146.",
      "impact": "serious",
      "confidence": 1.0
    }
  ]
}

{
  "translated_text": "中国的",
  "original_language": "zh",
  "translated_language": "en"
}

{
  "ruleId": "WAI-ARIA",
  "status": "Passed",
  "description": "This rule checks that WAI-ARIA states or properties are allowed for the element they are specified on.",
  "applicability": {
    "appliesTo": [
      "any WAI-ARIA state or property"
    ],
    "on": [
      "HTML or SVG elements",
      "included in the accessibility tree"
    ]
  },
  "expectation": {
    "global": true,
    "semanticRole": true,
    "languageFeature": true
  },
  "assumptions": "",
  "accessibilitySupport": {
    "description": "Implementation of Presentational Roles Conflict Resolution varies from one browser or assistive technology to another. Depending on this, some elements can have a semantic role of 'none' and their attributes fail this rule with some technologies but users of other technology would not experience any accessibility issue."
  },
  "background": {
    "description": "In HTML, there are language features that do not have corresponding implicit WAI-ARIA semantics. As per ARIA in HTML Editor's Draft, those elements can have global states or properties. Some of those elements can also have inherited, supported, or required states or properties that correspond to a WAI-ARIA role."
  },
  "relatedRules": [
    {
      "ruleId": "WAI-ARIA",
      "status": "Passed"
    }
  ]
}

{
  "violations": [
    {
      "wcagCode": "G146",
      "violationName": "Canvas as the Only Means of Presentation",
      "category": "layout",
      "html": "<canvas id=\"logo\" width=\"72\" height=\"48\" aria-label=\"HTML 5 logo\"></canvas>",
      "target": "#logo",
      "description": "The canvas element is used for rendering an image, but it does not provide any alternative text or a fallback mechanism. This violates WCAG 2.0 Success Criterion G146.",
      "impact": "serious",
      "confidence": 1.0
    }
  ]
}

{
  "response": "The provided text appears to be a snippet of HTML, CSS, and JavaScript code related to a user interface component. It includes elements such as dropdown menus for language selection, translation feedback forms, and popup overlays. The code is designed to handle interactions like language switching, form submissions, and managing the visibility of UI components using event listeners and custom functions."

  }

{
  "id": "WCAG21-AriaRules-4.1.2",
  "title": "Non-repeated Content has a Heading",
  "description": "This rule checks that the non-repeated content contains a heading.",
  "applicability": {
    "subject": "HTML web page"
  },
  "expectations": [
    "In each test target, either there is no non-repeated content after repeated content or there exists an element for which all of the following are true:",
    "- The element is non-repeated content after repeated content.",
    "- The element is a semantic heading.",
    "- The element is visible.",
    "- The element is included in the accessibility tree."
  ],
  "assumptions": [
    "This rule assumes that headings used to pass Technique H69: Providing heading elements at the beginning of each section of content have to be included in the accessibility tree in order to be beneficial to users of assistive technologies.",
    "This rule assumes that the first non-repeated content is starting a new section of content. If this is not the case, it is possible to fail the rule while still passing Technique H69: Providing heading elements at the beginning of each section of content."
  ],
  "accessibilitySupport": [
    "Having a heading for the non-repeated content is sufficient to pass Success Criterion 2.4.1 Bypass blocks. However, if headings are used for that goal, they will only benefit users who can actually navigate from heading to heading (such as functionality provided by browsers, browser plugins, screen readers or other assistive technologies). Users without any possibility of headings navigation will be left without a way of bypassing blocks of repeated content and will still experience accessibility issues. Therefore, it is recommended to provide other ways of bypassing blocks.",
    "When headings are rendered without sufficient visual cues, they are not perceived as headings by sighted users. In this case, passing this rule might still fail Technique H69: Providing heading elements at the beginning of each section of content and Success Criterion 2.4.1 Bypass blocks. Additionally, this is likely a failure of Success Criterion 1.3.1 Info and Relationships."
  ],
  "background": {
    "intention": "The intention of this rule is that the heading is at (or near) the start of the main area of content of a document.",
    "ambiguity": "Defining the main area of content in a non-ambiguous way is not really doable. Therefore, the rule takes a more lenient position and only requires the heading to be some non-repeated content."
  },
  "bibliography": [
    {
      "title": "Success Criterion 2.4.1 Bypass blocks",
      "url": "https://www.w3.org/TR/WCAG21/#bypass-blocks"
    },
    {
      "title": "Technique H69: Providing heading elements at the beginning of each section of content",
      "url": "https://www.w3.org/WAI/WCAG21/Techniques/html/H69"
    }
  ]
}

